@page "/"
@using Fhi.Munin.Explorer.Blazor
@using Fhi.Munin.Explorer.Contracts
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<PageTitle>Modern host</PageTitle>

<div class="hostchrome">
    <p class="hostchrome-note">
        This page is a routed <code>.razor</code> page in a modern Blazor Web App. Everything
        inside the frame below is the <code>Fhi.Munin.Explorer.Blazor</code> package, mounted the
        modern way — a component tag with <code>@@rendermode</code> set here, by the host, inside
        an app served by <code>MapRazorComponents&lt;App&gt;()</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="hostchrome-note">
        <code>samples/LegacyHost</code> mounts the very same component the other way — a
        <code>&lt;component&gt;</code> tag helper in an MVC view, no router, no
        <code>@@rendermode</code> — because that is what helsedata.no's Optimizely CMS does. The
        component has to work in both, so both hosts are kept alive.
    </p>
    <p class="hostchrome-note">
        The package ships a second root component beside this one — <code>KildeExplorer</code>, the
        kildeutforsker. It has a page of its own: <a href="/kilder">kilder</a>. Ticking kilder
        there and pressing <em>Utforsk variabler for utvalget</em> arrives back here with the ids
        in the query, which is the whole of the handover between the two.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="hostframe">
    @* render-mode is set HERE, by the host. The component itself is render-mode free.
       prerender: false for the same reason LegacyHost uses render-mode="Server" rather than
       "ServerPrerendered" — the component fetches on initialise, and prerendering would run
       that call twice against the public API on every page load. *@
    <VariableSearch Language="no"
                    Filter="_filter"
                    @rendermode="@(new InteractiveServerRenderMode(prerender: false))" />
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The kilder the query names, which is how a link out of Kelda arrives.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Read once here rather than bound: this page mirrors nothing back out, unlike LegacyHost's
    /// wrapper, which is the sample that shows the whole shareable-state contract. What this shows
    /// is the receiving half of the handover, and it is deliberately the smallest thing that works
    /// — <c>VariableFilter.Parse</c> reads the very query <c>ToQueryString</c> wrote on /kilder, so
    /// neither page restates the format.
    /// <para>
    /// The filter crosses from this statically-rendered page into the interactive island as a
    /// component parameter, so it is serialised on the way. That works because <c>VariableFilter</c>
    /// is a record of primitives and lists of them; a parameter type that were not would fail here
    /// and nowhere else in this repository, since LegacyHost hands its filter to a wrapper that is
    /// itself the interactive component and so never crosses a boundary at all.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private VariableFilter _filter = VariableFilter.None;

    protected override void OnInitialized() =>
        _filter = VariableFilter.Parse(new Uri(Nav.Uri).Query);
}
